// Robots in Waldo, driven over MCP and from the stack's headset. The
// simulation starts empty; robots are named at launch, joined later or added
// over MCP, and each comes up with its rendered rig, listed on the MCP
// endpoint and paired into the headset. Three endpoints, one per family: a
// call to the framework, to the simulated world and to a robot each goes to
// its own endpoint:
//
//   http://127.0.0.1:8903/framework_controls/v1/mcp   the peppy framework: the stack of nodes, where a client adds a robot of this file's options and removes it
//   http://127.0.0.1:8902/simulation/v1/mcp           the simulated world: the scene, its objects' controls, its light sources, its materials, its views, its clock, where a robot can work in it
//   http://127.0.0.1:8900/robot_control/v1/mcp        every robot of the stack: its identity, moves, limbs, cameras, workspace, brain and recorder, each call naming its robot
//
// The robots' endpoint is the robot_control axis every launcher with robots
// carries; this file deploys it, and the other launchers serve it with
// `--with robot_control`. Every robot beside it is listed with its identity,
// its limb state, its moves and where its design lets it work, with its
// recorder whenever it runs, its rig's cameras and its brain's tools, at
// launch or by a later join, and a removal takes them out. The framework's
// endpoint comes with it, on the
// same option of this file alone: stack.list, stack.join and stack.remove
// add and remove the robots of the scope the adjustment below gives, the
// two robot options of this file with the features each offers, up to four
// robots on the stack, each robot and each feature on the machine of the
// federation the client names. The launch builds on its machine the nodes
// of each of these robots with each set of its features before it starts
// anything, so a robot or a feature that a client adds on this machine
// builds nothing, and one on another machine builds there when it joins;
// `--no-prebuild` builds only what the launch starts. The world's endpoint is this file's own
// world_control axis, deployed with a `none` option: Waldo alone implements
// the object controls, lighting, materials, view, clock and workspace
// contracts it binds, so the constraint below refuses it under any other
// engine, and `--with mujoco,world_control=none` is the robots' and the
// framework's endpoints under MuJoCo. The three URLs are the same with no
// robot, one or ten and before and after any join, so a client registers
// them once, and all three run while no robot stands in the simulation.
//
// The stack's headset is this file's headset axis, deployed with a `none`
// option: one xr_commander that every robot without a commander of its own
// pairs into, its page opened from Waldo's headset panel; the robot it
// drives is chosen on that page. An MCP client and the headset can drive the
// same robot: the backbone refuses a client's goal on a limb the headset is
// streaming until the grip is released, and a goal already in flight keeps its
// limb.
//
// What this file adds to openarm_simulation: the robots' and the
// framework's endpoints deployed, the world's endpoint on its own axis, the
// headset deployed, rendering on from the launch, no robot listed, and its
// robot entries: for each option no commander of its own and the rendered
// rig, so every robot named on the command line, at launch with `--join
// OPTION:NAME` or later with a plain `stack join`, and every robot
// stack.join adds, comes up driven over MCP and from the headset with its
// cameras, listed on the same URL when the join returns. A
// launch or a join of a simulated robot returns only once the simulation
// stands the robot. A robot that stack.join adds stands on a free floor
// spot, and workspace.stand_at on the world's endpoint stands it at a work
// surface. A v1 OpenArm, which the simulations render no rig for, joins with
// `--with v1,camera_rig=none`. The stacks this file is meant for, and the
// same client on the real robots, are in mcp/README.md:
//
//   peppy stack launch simulation_mcp                                    # Waldo and the three endpoints, no robot
//   peppy stack launch simulation_mcp --join openarm_sim:alpha           # an OpenArm v2 over MCP
//   peppy stack launch simulation_mcp --join openarm_sim:alpha,so101_sim:charlie   # an OpenArm and an SO-101
//   peppy stack launch simulation_mcp --with mujoco,world_control=none   # the robots' and the framework's endpoints, under MuJoCo
//   peppy stack join openarm_sim:bravo                                   # an OpenArm, on the same URL when the join returns
//   peppy stack join openarm_sim:delta --with ai_brain_vla@cn-gpu-box    # an OpenArm here, its brain on the machine cn-gpu-box
//   peppy stack launch simulation_mcp --join openarm_sim:xiaozhu --with xiaozhu.ai_brain_vla@cn-gpu-box   # an OpenArm named at launch, its brain on cn-gpu-box
//   peppy stack remove alpha                                             # the endpoints keep running
//
// A `--with` word on a join wins on its own axis and the entry's other axes
// stay selected: `--with web_commander` puts that robot under its own browser
// panel; the headset does not drive it. `--join openarm_sim:alpha --with
// robot_control=none,teleop=none,alpha.web_commander,alpha.lerobot_recorder`
// on the launch is the world's endpoint alone, alpha under the browser panel
// with the recorder filming its rig. A robot runs on the machine its name is
// placed on, `OPTION:NAME@CORE_NODE`, else on the coordinator, and a word
// with a machine runs the part of the robot it selects there:
// `ai_brain_vla@cn-gpu-box` runs the brain on cn-gpu-box, and
// `NAME.ai_brain_vla@cn-gpu-box` does so at launch.
{
  peppy_schema: "launcher/v1",

  components: [
    {
      name: "simulation",
      cardinality: "one",
      provides: ["simulation_inst"],
      options: {
        mujoco: "../simulation/fragments/mujoco.json5",
        isaac_sim: "../simulation/fragments/isaac_sim.json5",
        waldo: "../simulation/fragments/waldo.json5",
      },
    },
    // The robots' MCP endpoint, and the framework's endpoint that adds and
    // removes the robots it drives; `--with robot_control=none` switches
    // both off. Every robot is listed on the robots' endpoint with its moves.
    {
      name: "robot_control",
      cardinality: "one",
      options: {
        robot_control: {
          fragments: ["fragments/framework_controls.json5", "fragments/robot_control.json5"],
        },
        none: "../common/fragments/none.json5",
      },
    },
    // The simulated world's MCP endpoint, deployed by this file; `--with
    // world_control=none` switches it off. It binds Waldo's object controls,
    // lighting, materials, view, clock and workspace contracts, so the
    // constraint below requires Waldo.
    {
      name: "world_control",
      cardinality: "one",
      options: {
        world_control: "fragments/world_control.json5",
        none: "../common/fragments/none.json5",
      },
    },
    // The stack's teleoperation headset, deployed by this file; `--with
    // teleop=none` switches it off. Every robot the endpoint adds runs no
    // commander of its own, so it pairs into the headset, which drives the
    // one chosen on its page.
    {
      name: "teleop",
      cardinality: "one",
      options: {
        xr_commander: "../teleop/fragments/xr_commander.json5",
        none: "../common/fragments/none.json5",
      },
    },
    // Every simulated robot, an option of the same name in every simulation
    // launcher. Every simulation stands any number of them, of any model.
    {
      name: "robot",
      cardinality: "zero_or_more",
      options: {
        openarm_sim: {
          fragments: ["../openarm/fragments/openarm_sim.json5"],
          adjustments: [
            // Listed on the MCP endpoint: who it is, what its limbs measure,
            // how close its arms stand, where its cameras are mounted, where
            // its design lets it work, and the moves a model drives it by.
            { target: "robot_control_inst",
              add_links: { identity: ["init_inst"], limb_state: ["backbone_inst"], collision: ["backbone_inst"],
                           camera_mounts: ["backbone_inst"], workspace: ["backbone_inst"],
                           postures: ["backbone_inst"], limb_motion: ["backbone_inst"] } },
            // The stack's headset leads a robot with no commander of its own:
            // the backbone pairs into it (the robot's fragment), and the
            // headset's buttons reach the robot's postures and recorder.
            { target: "headset_inst", when: { teleop: "xr_commander", robot_commander: "none" },
              add_links: { postures: ["backbone_inst"] } },
            { target: "headset_inst", when: { teleop: "xr_commander", robot_commander: "none", recorder: "lerobot_recorder" },
              add_links: { recorder: ["recorder_inst"] } },
            // The panel streams the live cap the simulated backbone starts on.
            { target: "commander_inst", when: { robot_commander: "web_commander" },
              set_arguments: { max_ee_velocity_m_s: 0.5 } },
            // An episode also starts from the MCP endpoint.
            { target: "robot_control_inst", when: { recorder: "lerobot_recorder" },
              add_links: { recorder: ["recorder_inst"] } },
            // Dataset identity, by hardware version and engine.
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { hardware_version: "v1", simulation: "mujoco" },
              set_arguments: { robot_type: "openarm_v1_mujoco", storage_root: "/tmp/lerobot_openarm_mujoco" } },
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { hardware_version: "v1", simulation: "isaac_sim" },
              set_arguments: { robot_type: "openarm_v1_isaac", storage_root: "/tmp/lerobot_openarm_isaac" } },
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { hardware_version: "v1", simulation: "waldo" },
              set_arguments: { robot_type: "openarm_v1_waldo", storage_root: "/tmp/lerobot_openarm_waldo" } },
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { hardware_version: "v2", simulation: "mujoco" },
              set_arguments: { robot_type: "openarm_v2_mujoco", storage_root: "/tmp/lerobot_openarm_mujoco" } },
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { hardware_version: "v2", simulation: "isaac_sim" },
              set_arguments: { robot_type: "openarm_v2_isaac", storage_root: "/tmp/lerobot_openarm_isaac" } },
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { hardware_version: "v2", simulation: "waldo" },
              set_arguments: { robot_type: "openarm_v2_waldo", storage_root: "/tmp/lerobot_openarm_waldo" } },
            // The brain's tools, listed beside the moves.
            { target: "robot_control_inst", when: { brain: ["ai_brain_vla", "vlm_router_brain"] },
              add_links: { item_perception: ["brain_inst"], item_manipulation: ["brain_inst"], brain_model: ["brain_inst"] } },
            // The simulated robot's brain sees through the chest camera the
            // rendered rig binds, color and depth from one producer; a brain
            // on another machine than the simulation's reads it over the
            // network. ai_brain_vla finds and names items with the SAM 3 and
            // SigLIP pipeline, by words from the node's general vocabulary.
            // The pipeline runs in the brain's node, on the machine the
            // brain runs on: the robot's, or the one its word names
            // (`ai_brain_vla@cn-gpu-box`). It computes on that machine's
            // NVIDIA GPU, or slowly on its CPU when the machine has none, and
            // its first load on a machine downloads its weights there. Under
            // Waldo it also enrols the catalogue's table objects, the
            // prototypes the node ships, so a rendered mesh whose look the
            // words miss is still reported under the catalogue's name; this
            // is a condition of the simulation, not of the robot, whose brain
            // names by words alone. vlm_router_brain asks a hosted model
            // instead, with the key the launching shell exports, and needs
            // no GPU.
            // perception_model: the node's own default applies (see the node's peppy.json5, parameter perception_model).
            { target: "brain_inst", when: { brain: "ai_brain_vla", simulation: "waldo" },
              set_arguments: { perception_gallery: "waldo_catalogue" } },
            { target: "brain_inst", when: { brain: ["ai_brain_vla", "vlm_router_brain"], camera_rig: "cameras_sim" },
              set_links: { camera: "chest", geometry: "chest" } },
            // The recorder films the rig; binding order fixes the dataset's
            // feature order.
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { camera_rig: "cameras_sim" },
              add_links: { color_cameras: ["wrist_left", "wrist_right"], rgbd_cameras: ["chest"] } },
            // The stack's headset shows the rig in its panels while it leads
            // this robot.
            { target: "headset_inst", when: { teleop: "xr_commander", robot_commander: "none", camera_rig: "cameras_sim" },
              add_links: { color_cameras: ["wrist_left", "wrist_right"], rgbd_cameras: ["chest"] } },
            // Listed on the MCP endpoint with profile and geometry.
            { target: "robot_control_inst", when: { camera_rig: "cameras_sim" },
              add_links: { camera: ["wrist_left", "wrist_right"], depth_camera: ["chest"],
                           camera_profile: ["wrist_left", "wrist_right", "chest"],
                           camera_geometry: ["wrist_left", "wrist_right", "chest"] } },
            // The commander, recorder and brain are shared with the physical robot.
            { target: "commander_inst", set_framework: { clock: "simulation" } },
            { target: "recorder_inst", set_framework: { clock: "simulation" } },
            { target: "brain_inst", set_framework: { clock: "simulation" } },
          ],
        },
        so101_sim: {
          fragments: ["../so101/fragments/so101_sim.json5"],
          adjustments: [
            // Listed on the MCP endpoint: who it is, what its limbs measure,
            // where its design lets it work, and the moves a model drives it by.
            { target: "robot_control_inst",
              add_links: { identity: ["init_inst"], limb_state: ["backbone_inst"], workspace: ["backbone_inst"],
                           postures: ["backbone_inst"], limb_motion: ["backbone_inst"] } },
            // The stack's headset leads a robot with no commander of its own:
            // the backbone pairs into its right hand (the robot's fragment),
            // and the headset's buttons reach the robot's postures and
            // recorder.
            { target: "headset_inst", when: { teleop: "xr_commander", robot_commander: "none" },
              add_links: { postures: ["backbone_inst"] } },
            { target: "headset_inst", when: { teleop: "xr_commander", robot_commander: "none", recorder: "lerobot_recorder" },
              add_links: { recorder: ["recorder_inst"] } },
            // An episode also starts from the MCP endpoint.
            { target: "robot_control_inst", when: { recorder: "lerobot_recorder" },
              add_links: { recorder: ["recorder_inst"] } },
            // Dataset identity, by engine.
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { simulation: "mujoco" },
              set_arguments: { robot_type: "so101_mujoco", storage_root: "/tmp/lerobot_so101_mujoco" } },
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { simulation: "isaac_sim" },
              set_arguments: { robot_type: "so101_isaac", storage_root: "/tmp/lerobot_so101_isaac" } },
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { simulation: "waldo" },
              set_arguments: { robot_type: "so101_waldo", storage_root: "/tmp/lerobot_so101_waldo" } },
            // The recorder films the rig; binding order fixes the dataset's
            // feature order.
            { target: "recorder_inst", when: { camera_rig: "cameras_sim" },
              add_links: { color_cameras: ["wrist"] } },
            // The stack's headset shows the rig in its panel while it leads
            // this robot.
            { target: "headset_inst", when: { teleop: "xr_commander", robot_commander: "none", camera_rig: "cameras_sim" },
              add_links: { color_cameras: ["wrist"] } },
            // Listed on the MCP endpoint with profile and geometry.
            { target: "robot_control_inst", when: { camera_rig: "cameras_sim" },
              add_links: { camera: ["wrist"], camera_profile: ["wrist"], camera_geometry: ["wrist"] } },
            // The commander and recorder are shared with the physical robot.
            { target: "commander_inst", set_framework: { clock: "simulation" } },
            { target: "recorder_inst", set_framework: { clock: "simulation" } },
          ],
        },
      },
    },
  ],

  // An entry's `with` selects the robot's own axes for every copy of the
  // option, and no entry lists a copy, so the bare launch stands no robot:
  // every copy named on the command line, at launch with `--join
  // OPTION:NAME` or later with `stack join`, and every copy stack.join adds
  // on the framework's endpoint, runs no commander of its own, pairs into
  // the stack's headset and films.
  deployments: [
    { simulation: "waldo" },
    { robot_control: "robot_control" },
    { world_control: "world_control" },
    { teleop: "xr_commander" },
    {
      robot: "openarm_sim",
      // A v1, which the simulations render no rig for, joins with
      // `stack join openarm_sim:bravo --with v1,camera_rig=none`.
      with: { robot_commander: "none", camera_rig: "cameras_sim" },
    },
    {
      robot: "so101_sim",
      with: { robot_commander: "none", camera_rig: "cameras_sim" },
    },
  ],

  adjustments: [
    // Every engine renders a camera only while a robot holds its pair, so
    // rendering is on from the launch and a joined robot's rig comes up.
    { target: "simulation_inst", set_arguments: { cameras_enabled: true } },
    // The server reads the simulation's clock, as every instance a robot
    // copy runs does; each simulated robot's fragment binds its own.
    { target: "robot_control_inst", set_framework: { clock: "simulation" } },
    // The stack's headset reads the simulation's clock like every instance
    // a robot copy runs, and Waldo's headset panel shows it: the link that
    // opens its page in the headset.
    { target: "headset_inst", set_framework: { clock: "simulation" } },
    { target: "simulation_inst", when: { simulation: "waldo", teleop: "xr_commander" },
      add_links: { headsets: ["headset_inst"] } },
    // The robots a client of the framework's endpoint can add: each robot
    // option of this file, with the model its robot initializer names, which
    // a client shows a picture of before it adds the robot, and one line for
    // the client that says what the robot is and how it stands, since no
    // tool reports a stance before the robot stands, up to four robots on
    // the stack. Every copy renders its cameras, and a furnished scene has
    // three work surfaces 1.5 m apart.
    // An option also lists its features: each selects one option of one of
    // the robot's own axes that brings a node, with one line that says what
    // it adds, so the client adds it when its task needs it. The OpenArm
    // offers its brain; the SO-101 has no brain axis, so it offers no
    // feature. stack.join adds a robot of an option with the features it
    // names, as `peppy stack join` does with their words, each on its
    // machine: the robot on the machine the client names for it, and each
    // feature on the machine the client names for that feature. So a brain
    // can run on another machine than its robot, for example one with an
    // NVIDIA GPU. stack.list lists each robot with its features and their
    // machines, whether the client or the command line added it.
    { target: "framework_controls_inst",
      set_daemon_scopes: { stack: {
        max_copies: 4,
        options: [
          { option: "openarm_sim", model: "openarm_v2",
            description: "A simulated OpenArm v2 standing on the floor: two 7-joint arms with grippers on a stand, a depth camera on its chest and a camera on each wrist",
            features: [
              { feature: "ai_brain", axis: "brain", option: "ai_brain_vla",
                description: "An AI brain that finds and names items by words, then grabs and places them; it runs slowly without an NVIDIA GPU on its machine" },
            ] },
          { option: "so101_sim", model: "so101",
            description: "A simulated SO-101 clamped on the edge of a table: one arm with a gripper and a camera on its wrist" },
        ],
      } } },
  ],

  constraints: [
    // A property of the world's document: its controls, lighting,
    // materials, view, clock, workspace and reset targets bind the
    // simulation, and Waldo is the one simulation implementing those
    // contracts.
    { when: { world_control: "world_control" },
      requires: [{ simulation: "waldo" }],
      reason: "the simulated world's endpoint binds object_controls, scene_lighting, scene_materials, scene_view, simulation_clock, scene_workspace and simulation_reset, which only waldo implements; launch with waldo or with world_control=none" },
  ],
}
